course: biology
lesson: 361
title: Growing up: growth spurts, height, voices and getting stronger
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Growing up is not one smooth climb: you grow slowly and steadily for years, then shoot up fast as a teenager, and then you stop for good.
explain: Picture the pencil marks on a kitchen door frame, one for every birthday: for years they creep up a hand's width apart, then suddenly one year the gap is twice as big.
explain: Bones get longer at growth plates, soft bands near their ends; growth hormone, made mostly in deep sleep, tells them to grow, and food gives them the bricks.
explain: Other things grow too: the voice box gets bigger and the voice drops, muscles get stronger, and the brain keeps rewiring itself until you are about 25.
fact: Between about 4 years old and the teenage years most children grow about 5 to 6 centimetres a year, and growth hormone is let out mostly during deep sleep at night.
fact: You are about a centimetre taller in the morning than at bedtime, because the soft discs between the bones of your back squash a little during the day.
fact: Growth plates are bands of soft cartilage near the ends of bones where new bone is added; they show as gaps on an X-ray because cartilage lets X-rays through, and they close into solid bone when the teenage years end.
fact: There are 27 bones in one hand and wrist, and doctors can guess a child's age from how the hand bones look on an X-ray.
fact: In the teenage growth spurt children can grow about 8 to 10 centimetres in a year; feet and hands grow first, then legs, then the body, and shoe size often stops changing a year or two before height.
fact: Height comes mostly from hundreds of genes from both parents, plus food, sleep and health; a body that is often hungry or ill grows less, which doctors call stunting.
fact: People in the Netherlands grew about 20 centimetres taller on average over about 150 years, mostly thanks to better food and health.
fact: For most people the arm span from fingertip to fingertip is about the same as their height, and Leonardo da Vinci drew a famous man in a circle and a square to show such proportions.
fact: A baby's head is about a quarter of its length, but a grown-up's head is only about an eighth of their height.
fact: In the teenage years the voice box grows and the vocal folds get longer and thicker, so the voice gets deeper and cracks while the brain learns to control them; the Adam's apple is the front of the voice box, and girls' voices drop a little too.
fact: Muscles get stronger mostly as each fibre gets thicker, but young children get stronger mainly because the brain learns to switch on more fibres at once.
fact: Many children aged about 3 to 12 get aching legs in the evening called growing pains; doctors are not sure growing bones cause them, and a limp, swelling, fever or pain in one spot means seeing a doctor.
fact: Bones need calcium, protein and vitamin D, which your skin makes in daylight; extra food cannot make you taller than your genes allow, it only makes you wider.
fact: The brain keeps rewiring until about 25, and the front part that plans and stops you doing silly things finishes last, which scientists think may be one reason teenagers take more risks.
exercise: About how much do most children grow each year between about 4 and the teenage years? | About 5 to 6 centimetres | About 1 millimetre, hardly anything | About 30 centimetres | About 1 metre
exercise: When is most growth hormone let out? | During deep sleep at night | During lunch at school, when you are eating | Only while you are running about | Only on your birthday each year
exercise: Why are you about a centimetre taller in the morning than at bedtime? | The soft discs in your back squash during the day and spring back at night | Your bones grow a whole centimetre longer every single night while you sleep in bed | Your feet swell up while you sleep | Your hair stands up taller in the morning
exercise: What are the gaps near the ends of the bones on a child's X-ray? | Growth plates of soft cartilage where bones get longer | Little cracks left from all the times the child fell over | Empty holes full of air | Places where bones are missing
exercise: What happens to growth plates when the teenage years finish? | They turn to solid bone and close, so you stop getting taller | They open wider and wider, so you grow faster than ever before | They fall out like baby teeth | They move to the middle of the bone
exercise: How many bones are in one hand and wrist? | 27 | 5 | 12 | 206
exercise: In the teenage growth spurt, which parts grow first? | The feet and hands | The head and the nose | The body and the chest | The ears and the eyes
exercise: About how much can a teenager grow in one year during the spurt? | About 8 to 10 centimetres | About 1 centimetre | About 50 centimetres | Nothing at all
exercise: What decides most of how tall you will be? | Hundreds of genes from both parents, plus food, sleep and health | One single height gene that you get only from your dad | Only how much milk you drink | How much you stretch every morning
exercise: Why are people in the Netherlands about 20 centimetres taller than 150 years ago? | Mostly better food and health | Their genes all changed in one go | They all sleep standing up now | They wear much taller shoes today
exercise: For most people, the arm span is about the same as what? | Their height | Twice their height | The length of one leg | The size of their head
exercise: About what part of a baby's length is its head? | About a quarter | Only about an eighth | About a half or more | Only about a tenth
exercise: Why does a teenage boy's voice get deeper? | The voice box grows and the vocal folds get longer and thicker | His tongue gets much bigger and heavier and fills more of his mouth | His lungs shrink a little | His teeth change shape
exercise: What is the Adam's apple? | The front of a grown voice box sticking out | A piece of apple that got stuck in the throat long ago | A bone that grows from the jaw | A muscle used for chewing
exercise: Why do young children mainly get stronger as they grow? | The brain learns to switch on more muscle fibres at once | Their muscles become twice as big every single year | Their bones turn into muscle | They grow extra muscles
exercise: Which is a sign that leg pain is not just growing pains? | A limp, swelling, fever or pain in only one spot | Both legs ache a little in the evening | The ache comes after a day of football | The ache is gone by the morning
exercise: Where does your body get most of its vitamin D? | Your skin makes it when daylight shines on it | Your bones suck it out of the air you breathe all day long | You breathe it in from the sea | Only from eating sweets
exercise: Which part of the brain finishes growing last? | The front part that plans and stops you doing silly things | The part at the back that controls your breathing and heartbeat | The part that sees colours | The part that hears music
test: «drink loads of milk and eat 5 dinners and you'll be taller than dad» says a friend. What is right? | Food builds the body, but extra food cannot make you taller than your genes allow | Yes, every extra dinner adds a centimetre | Yes, but only if the milk is cold | No, food has nothing to do with growing
test: Why might a doctor always measure a child at the same time of day? | Because you are about a centimetre taller in the morning than at night | Because rulers shrink in the afternoon | Because children only grow after lunch | Because the doctor's eyes get tired by the evening and cannot read the numbers
test: «bro my voice just went squeaky then deep in one sentence lol, am i ill?» What is the truth? | No, the vocal folds are growing and the brain has not learned to control them yet | Yes, it is a serious throat illness that needs a doctor and medicine straight away | Yes, you swallowed something stuck in your throat | No, it means you have stopped growing
test: Count them: a doctor looks at X-rays of both hands of a girl. How many bones in both hands and wrists together? | 54 | 27 | 10 | 206
test: A teenager suddenly keeps bumping into door frames and tripping over his own feet. What is a likely reason? | His hands, feet and legs grew fast and his brain has not caught up yet | His eyes have stopped working properly and he needs glasses for everything | His bones are getting shorter | His feet have stopped growing
test: «my great-great-grandad was way shorter than me, so height's not in your genes, right?» What is the best answer? | Genes matter, but better food and health let today's children reach more of their height | Right, genes have nothing to do with height | People got taller because they stretch more | People in the past were short because they walked a lot
test: In a cartoon, Tom stretches Jerry's arm until it is twice as long as he is tall, and it snaps back like elastic. What is true for a real body? | Arms grow slowly at growth plates, and the arm span stays about the same as the height | Arms really can stretch twice as long if pulled | Pulling arms every day makes them grow fast | Arms snap back because they are made of rubber
test: What would happen if a 20-year-old ate a huge amount of food every day for a year? | He would get wider, not taller, because his growth plates have closed | He would grow about 10 centimetres taller, as long as he ate plenty of milk and meat | His growth plates would open again | He would shrink a little
test: «small gymnast can do more pull-ups than my big dad, how?» Which is the best reason? | She is very strong for her small size, and pulling a light body is easier | She has more bones in her arms than he has, and extra bones make her stronger | Her muscles are bigger than his | Gymnasts have springs in their arms
test: «legs ache every night after footie, but only the left knee is swollen and I'm limping» What is the right thing to do? | Tell a grown-up and see a doctor, as swelling and a limp are not growing pains | Ignore it, it is only growing pains | Run more to make the swelling go | Drink milk and it will be fine
test: Why do babies look top-heavy compared with grown-ups? | A baby's head is about a quarter of its length, a grown-up's only an eighth | A baby's head is filled with air | Babies have shorter necks only because they are lazy and do not hold their heads up | Babies' heads stop growing at birth
test: A child in a sunny country plays outside every day. Why might she need fewer vitamin D drops than a child in a dark, cloudy country? | Her skin makes vitamin D in the daylight | Sunlight puts extra calcium into her food | Vitamin D drops do not work in hot places | Children in sunny countries never grow at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an adult person cannot increase in stature by the consumption of additional nourishment. | The growth plates of the bones have closed and become solid bone | The stomach declines to digest food after the age of twenty | Nourishment is employed solely in the growth of hair | The skeleton is dissolved by excess food
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the deepening of the voice during adolescence? | The larynx enlarges and the vocal folds become longer and thicker | The tongue enlarges considerably and thereby obstructs the passage of the throat | The lungs diminish in capacity | The teeth are replaced by larger teeth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the secretion of growth hormone in children. | It is released principally during deep sleep at night | It is released exclusively during physical exercise | It is released solely upon the consumption of milk | It is released only upon the attainment of adulthood
exam: It is given that a child measured at bedtime is found to be shorter than when measured upon waking. Ascertain the reason. | The intervertebral discs are compressed in the course of the day | The bones are shortened by the evening meal | The measuring instrument expands overnight | The child grows one centimetre each night permanently
